'''''[[Strictly Come Dancing]]''''' returned for its '''nineteenth series''' with a launch show on 18 September 2021 on BBC One, and the live shows beginning on 25 September.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.radiotimes.com/tv/entertainment/strictly-come-dancing-2021-launch-date-confirmed-newsupdate/|title=Strictly Come Dancing 2021 launch date is officially confirmed|work=[[Radio Times]]|last=Carr|first=Flora|date=3 September 2021|access-date=8 September 2021}}</ref> [[Tess Daly]] and [[Claudia Winkleman]] returned as hosts, while [[Rylan Clark]] returned to host ''[[Strictly Come Dancing: It Takes Two|Strictly: It Takes Two]]'' alongside new presenter [[Janette Manrara]], who replaced [[Zoe Ball]].<ref name="presenters">{{Cite web|title=Janette Manrara to join BBC Two's Strictly: It Takes Two as new host|url=http://bbc.com/mediacentre/2021/janette-manrara-strictly-it-takes-two/|access-date=2021-06-10|website=bbc.com|language=en}}</ref> In June 2021, the [[BBC]] announced that [[Shirley Ballas]], [[Motsi Mabuse]], and [[Craig Revel Horwood]] would return to the judging panel. [[Anton Du Beke]], who served as a guest judge in the [[Strictly Come Dancing series 18|previous series]], joined the panel in place of [[Bruno Tonioli]], who did not return for the second year in a row due to travel restrictions imposed by the [[COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/webarchive/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.bbc.co.uk%2Fblogs%2Fstrictlycomedancing%2Fentries%2F7b8eaad9-0009-4b62-afaa-ac7c338eb6f1|title=Judges revealed for Strictly 2021!|website=[[BBC]]|date=2021-06-24|access-date=2021-06-24}}</ref><ref name=judges>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-57592468|title=Anton Du Beke to join Strictly judges for new series|work=BBC News|date=24 June 2021}}</ref>

In July 2021, the show's executive producer, Sarah James, said the show would return for a "full-length series", with fifteen celebrities, themed weeks, pre-recorded professional group dances, and a Christmas special, but no Blackpool [[Blackpool Tower#Tower Ballroom|Tower Ballroom]] special.<ref name="beginning">{{Cite web |last=Methven |first=Nicola |date=27 July 2021 |title=Strictly Come Dancing fans to get 'full fix' this year apart from Blackpool week |url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/tv/tv-news/strictly-come-dancing-full-fix-24630687 |website=mirror}}</ref> For this series, the contestants were no longer restricted to perform one of the "couple's choice" categories — [[Contemporary dance|contemporary]], [[Jazz dance|theatre/jazz]], or [[Street dance|street/commercial]] — and could instead incorporate the routine however they wished.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/webarchive/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.bbc.co.uk%2Fblogs%2Fstrictlycomedancing%2Fentries%2F98e84a5d-4b13-4ba2-9ce8-8e3872a638d3|title=Week Eight is going to be great!|website=[[BBC]]|date=13 November 2021|access-date=13 November 2021}}</ref> In September, a row broke out over whether dancers should be vaccinated against [[COVID-19]]. Reports had suggested that three dancers had refused to be vaccinated, with one unnamed celebrity already having to self-isolate since the show's launch night had been filmed.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.walesonline.co.uk/whats-on/whats-on-news/strictly-come-dancings-craig-revel-21678843|title=Strictly's Craig Revel Horwood says not having Covid vaccine is 'ridiculous'|first=Matt|last=Jackson|date=25 September 2021|website=WalesOnline}}</ref>

''[[EastEnders]]'' actress [[Rose Ayling-Ellis]] and [[Giovanni Pernice]] were announced as the winners on 18 December,<ref>{{cite web |author=PA Media |date=18 December 2021 |title=History made as Strictly Come Dancing winners crowned |url=https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2021/dec/18/history-made-as-strictly-come-dancing-winners-crowned |accessdate=19 December 2021 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB}}</ref> while [[John Whaite]] and [[Johannes Radebe]] — the first male same-sex couple to compete on the programme — finished in second place.

