The couples dance each week in a live show. The judges score each performance out of ten. The couples are then ranked according to the judges' scores and given points according to their rank, with the lowest scored couple receiving one point, and the highest scored couple receiving the most points (the maximum number of points available depends on the number of couples remaining in the competition). The public are also invited to vote for their favourite couples, and the couples are ranked again according to the number of votes they receive, again receiving points; the couple with the fewest votes receiving one point, and the couple with the most votes receiving the most points.

The points for judges' score and public vote are then added together, and the two couples with the fewest points are placed in the bottom two. If two couples have equal points, the points from the public vote are given precedence. As with the previous series, the bottom two couples have to perform a dance-off on the results show. Based on that performance alone, each judge then votes on which couple should stay and which couple should leave, with [[Shirley Ballas]], as head judge, having the last and [[Casting vote|deciding vote]].

==Professional dancers==
In March 2021, the [[BBC]] announced that all sixteen professional dancers from the eighteenth series would return in 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Strictly Come Dancing's 2021 professional dancers revealed |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/mediacentre/2021/strictly-professional-dancers-revealed |website=www.bbc.co.uk}}</ref> However, in June, the BBC revealed that [[Janette Manrara]] and [[Anton Du Beke]] would not be reprising their dancing roles: Manrara was announced as a new co-host of ''[[Strictly Come Dancing: It Takes Two|It Takes Two]]'', succeeding [[Zoe Ball]], while Du Beke would replace [[Bruno Tonioli]] on the judging panel.<ref name=presenters /><ref name=judges />

In July 2021, the BBC announced that four new professional dancers would be joining the cast: Italian Latin and Ballroom Champion [[Nikita Kuzmin]], who was also a professional dancer on the [[Let's Dance (German TV series)|German version of the show]] in 2020; South African Latin Champion Cameron Lombard, who was also a finalist on ''[[SA's Got Talent|South Africa's Got Talent]]'' in 2012; Polish Open Latin Champion [[Jowita Przystał]], who won ''[[The Greatest Dancer]]'' in 2020; and World Junior Latin American Champion [[Kai Widdrington]], who was also a former professional on the [[Dancing with the Stars (Irish TV series)|Irish version of the show]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/mediacentre/2021/new-strictly-pros-announced-2021|title=Four new professional dancers announced for Strictly Come Dancing 2021|website=www.bbc.co.uk}}</ref>

[[Graziano Di Prima]] received a celebrity partner for the first time since the [[Strictly Come Dancing (series 16)|sixteenth series]], while [[Nadiya Bychkova]] and [[Neil Jones (dancer)|Neil Jones]] received a celebrity partner for the first time since the [[Strictly Come Dancing (series 17)|seventeenth series]]. [[Nancy Xu]] received a celebrity partner for the first time, as did Nikita Kuzmin and Kai Widdrington, while [[Luba Mushtuk]], Cameron Lombard, and Jowita Przystał did not receive a partner.<ref name="couples">{{cite web|url=https://www.darlingtonandstocktontimes.co.uk/news/national/uk-today/19591439.couples-strictly-come-dancing-2021/|title=Who are the couples for Strictly Come Dancing 2021? See the full list|work=[[Darlington & Stockton Times]]|first=Carlo|last=Simone|date=19 September 2021|accessdate=20 September 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.express.co.uk/showbiz/tv-radio/1493277/Strictly-snub-luba-Mushtuk-no-celebrity-partner|title=Strictly snub: Why did Luba Mushtuk not get a celebrity partner?|work=[[Daily Express]]|first=Charlie|last=Milward|date=18 September 2021|accessdate=20 September 2021}}</ref>

==Couples==
This series featured fifteen celebrity contestants. On 4 August 2021, the first three celebrities participating were announced: [[Tom Fletcher]], [[AJ Odudu]], and [[Robert Webb]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/webarchive/https%3A%2F%2Fwww.bbc.co.uk%2Fblogs%2Fstrictlycomedancing%2Fentries%2F65bfb869-3a3e-4698-9304-d496d9952121|title=Meet our first three Strictly celebrities of 2021!|website=BBC One|date=4 August 2021|access-date=4 August 2021|language=en}}</ref> The following day, it was announced that [[John Whaite]] would be paired as part of the first male same-sex couple in ''[[Strictly Come Dancing]]'' history.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-58089932|title=Strictly Come Dancing: John Whaite to be in first all-male partnership|publisher=[[BBC News]]|date=5 August 2021|access-date=5 August 2021|language=en}}</ref> Celebrity contestants continued to be revealed until 13 August 2021, when the full line-up was announced.<ref name="couples"/> [[Rose Ayling-Ellis]] was also the first deaf contestant to appear on the programme.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/entertainment-arts-59599740|accessdate=15 January 2024|title=Strictly Come Dancing 2021: Rose Ayling-Ellis becomes first deaf winner|work=[[BBC]]|publisher=BBC|date=19 December 2023}}</ref> On 13 October, it was announced that Robert Webb had been advised to withdraw from the competition due to health problems.<ref name="robert">{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-58905864|title=Robert Webb withdraws from Strictly Come Dancing|date=13 October 2021|website=BBC News|access-date=13 October 2021}}</ref> On 17 December, the day before the final, it was announced that [[AJ Odudu]] had withdrawn from the competition after suffering ligament damage in her right ankle.<ref name="aj">{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/entertainment-arts-59696209|title=Strictly Come Dancing: AJ Odudu ruled out of final with ankle injury|date=17 December 2021|website=BBC News|access-date=17 December 2021}}</ref>

